window.__hvBdRegister("maximus-dissertations", {"key":"maximus-dissertations","sections":[{"i":0,"j":28,"loc":"Dissertation I","title":"What God is according to Plato","summary":"Maximus says that speaking of God is harder than speaking of daemons, and likens a first reading of Plato to digging up crude gold that reason must then refine by fire. Amid every disagreement about law and justice, all peoples agree that there is one God, king and father of all, with many gods his sons ruling with him; even the few atheists cannot escape the thought. An Academic interpreter explains that the soul has intellect and senses, and that God belongs to the stable intelligible realm: dividing the animate from the inanimate, and the rational from the irrational, reason climbs to an intellect that understands always, all things, at once. God is invisible and ineffable, reached only when the soul turns from the senses, led by reason and love, beyond the heavens to the plain of truth, fully at death. Those too weak for this vision may revere his offspring, the countless gods and daemons, set out like the ranks of a great king's empire.","ideas":["Universal agreement among Greeks and barbarians on one supreme God and many subordinate gods","Ascent by division from inanimate things to an intellect that knows all at once","God is the source of beauty, seen by stripping away all bodily attributes","The cosmos as a kingdom: the king, his attendant gods, and lesser ministers"],"terms":[{"t":"nous (intellect)","d":"The simple power of the soul that grasps intelligibles, as distinct from the many-sided, changing senses."},{"t":"the supercelestial place","d":"The region beyond the heavens, the plain of truth, where the soul's journey to God ends."},{"t":"daemons","d":"Divine beings in the aether, part of the countless offspring of God beneath the king."}]},{"i":29,"j":49,"loc":"Dissertation II","title":"Whether injuries should be returned","summary":"Correcting Pindar's doubt whether men rise by justice or by fraud, Maximus argues that the just man rises by justice and cannot in fact be injured. An injury is the taking away of good, and virtue, the only true good, cannot be taken; so the good man neither injures, by choice, nor is injured, by his virtue, while the bad man injures in intent but has no good to lose. Returning an injury is as base as starting one, and a law of retaliation would set off an endless chain of evil, as in the mutual abductions behind the Trojan War and the wars of Athens and Sparta. Socrates is the model: Anytus and Melitus could kill him but not harm him. The good man treats the penalties of the unjust as a grown man treats the fines of a children's court.","ideas":["Injury defined as removal of good; virtue cannot be removed","Wrongdoing judged by intent, as the law punishes attempted crimes","Retaliation multiplies evils without end, shown from Greek history","The just man laughs at the punishments of the unjust as at children's decrees"],"terms":[{"t":"adikia (injustice, injury)","d":"Here the intended removal of another's good, which harms the doer more than the victim."}]},{"i":50,"j":70,"loc":"Dissertation III","title":"Divination, fate and human choice","summary":"Starting from the oracle of the wooden wall and Themistocles' reading of it, Maximus asks whether divination leaves anything in our power. He holds that divine and human intellect are akin, differing as sunlight from firelight, and that the god does not answer idle curiosity. Images of machines moving great weights, an army under its general, and a ship under its pilot show how fate, human counsel and prophecy work together; oracles such as Laius's and Croesus's foretell the consequences of free acts rather than compelling them. Human life is amphibious, mixing liberty with necessity, like a chained man who follows willingly. He ends by blaming human evils not on Fate or the Furies, which he treats as pretexts, but on vices within the soul, whose outcomes Socrates could foretell as well as Apollo.","ideas":["Divination and human reasoning as allied forms of intellect","Oracles state conditional consequences, leaving choice intact","Life is amphibious, a mixture of freedom and necessity","Fate and the Furies are excuses for evils that spring from vice"],"terms":[{"t":"heimarmenē (fate)","d":"The order of necessity that human choice is mingled with; Maximus doubts the name and refuses to make it the cause of evil."},{"t":"mantikē (the prophetic art)","d":"The art that foresees what will follow from given conditions, like a pilot's or physician's foresight."}]},{"i":71,"j":87,"loc":"Dissertation IV","title":"Telling a flatterer from a friend","summary":"Maximus adapts Prodicus's fable of Heracles at the crossroads, replacing Pleasure with a smiling Flatterer and Virtue with a plain-spoken Friend. He tests and rejects several criteria for telling them apart: the outcome comes too late, and pleasure and pain, or gain and loss, are found on both sides, as physicians and fathers cause pain and Odysseus forced his men from the Lotus-eaters. The real difference lies in intention and disposition: the friend seeks a shared and equal good and frank speech, the flatterer his own advantage and servility. He extends the contrast to piety versus superstition, kingship versus tyranny, aristocracy versus democracy, the courtiers of Xerxes and Alexander, and finally to false arts that flatter true ones, ending with the sophist as the most exact flatterer of the philosopher.","ideas":["Prodicus's choice of Heracles recast as Friend versus Flatterer","Neither pleasure nor profit separates them; intention and use do","Friendship rests on equality, flattery on servility and gain","Spurious music, cookery-medicine and sophistry as flatteries of real arts"],"terms":[{"t":"kolax (flatterer)","d":"One who seeks his own pleasure or profit under the guise of friendship."},{"t":"deisidaimonia (superstition)","d":"Fearful flattery of the gods, contrasted with the pious man's friendship with them."}]},{"i":88,"j":111,"loc":"Dissertation V","title":"The case for the practical life","summary":"Observing that no one is content with his own way of life, and that even philosophers quarrel over theirs, Maximus calls the practical and theoretical lives to plead their cases, and the practical life speaks first. It pictures a city founder admitting each craft by what it contributes and asks what the contemplative brings, comparing him to an idle passenger on a ship and to a limb that refuses to work. Knowledge that leads to no action is buried treasure; Nestor, Odysseus, Heracles, and Zeus himself, whose government of the world never rests, are wise in deeds. The speaker answers the fear of danger with Cyrus, Plato's journeys for Dion, Xenophon's command, and Diogenes, and closes by arguing that retreat from the fray only invites attack, as it did for Socrates.","ideas":["Each citizen must bring a contribution, like parts of the body","Unused knowledge is like buried, useless wealth","Heracles and Zeus as models of wisdom expressed in ceaseless action","Withdrawal does not give safety; Socrates' sheltering wall could be taken"],"terms":[{"t":"bios praktikos (practical life)","d":"The life of civic and military action, here defended as the better life."}]},{"i":112,"j":131,"loc":"Dissertation VI","title":"The case for the contemplative life","summary":"The contemplative side now answers, in the person of Anaxagoras accused by the Clazomenians of deserting civic life. He argues that he has harmed no one, that power without sound judgment ruins its holder, and that cities are kept safe not by walls or docks but by harmony, which comes from laws, virtue, education and ultimately the study of truth in leisure. Before a judge chosen for knowledge, the philosopher would say that the soul is a city of three parts, and that the theoretical life matches its ruling part and kingship, the practical life the second rank, like aristocracy. Reason gives action priority in use but contemplation priority as the source of beauty, and assigns each by nature, age and fortune, as Plato and Xenophon acted when young and studied when old. The section ends praising the philosopher's mental journey through earth and heavens above all the travels of Odysseus.","ideas":["Anaxagoras's defence: truth sought in leisure is the root of civic virtue","The tripartite soul compared to kingship, aristocracy and mob rule","Action first in use, contemplation first as the cause of beauty","The soul's flight through the cosmos surpasses any earthly journey"],"terms":[{"t":"bios theōrētikos (contemplative life)","d":"The life given to the study of truth, here ranked above the practical life."},{"t":"ochlocracy","d":"Mob rule, which Maximus says is what is falsely called democracy."}]},{"i":132,"j":145,"loc":"Dissertation VII","title":"Why Plato excluded Homer from his city","summary":"Maximus opens with the Spartans expelling the famous cook Mithaecus, who was still welcomed elsewhere, and notes that different peoples rightly cultivate different arts. Plato's republic was a city built in theory for exactness rather than general use, like a statue combining the best of many bodies; just as a maker of perfect bodies would send away the physician without despising medicine, Plato sends away Homer without disgrace. Homer's two gifts, usefulness and pleasure, have no place there, since its education admits nothing chance or licentious, and pleasure must be stopped at its source. Maximus adds that many good cities, and virtuous barbarians, never knew Homer, and that Homer's verse is beautiful but, like musical modes, not suited to every use.","ideas":["Mithaecus expelled from Sparta yet honoured elsewhere","Plato's city aims at accuracy, so it can dismiss Homer without scorning him","The ideal city shuts out pleasure at its source","Homer's beauty is real but, like each musical mode, fitted to its use"],"terms":[{"t":"politeia (polity)","d":"Plato's city constructed in theory, governed by strict education rather than custom."}]},{"i":146,"j":171,"loc":"Dissertation VIII","title":"The amatory art of Socrates questioned","summary":"Stories of violent love (Actaeon of Corinth, Periander) and of just love (Harmodius, Epaminondas's sacred band at Thebes) show that one word names both a god and a disease. Maximus then raises the puzzle of Socrates' claims to be skilled in love and stirred by Charmides and Alcibiades, and states the critic's charge: that Socrates banished Homer for unseemly myths yet used riskier erotic language himself. In reply he notes that even Anytus, Melitus and Aristophanes never accused Socrates' loves, and that Socrates himself credited Sappho, Anacreon and Diotima. He traces the art of love back to Homer, who portrays every kind of love, from Achilles and Patroclus to Paris and Hector with Andromache, and draws close parallels between Socrates and Sappho, ending with Anacreon's chaste praise of beauty.","ideas":["Love is twofold: one kind aims at virtue, the other is a disease","The charge that Socrates' erotic talk is more dangerous than Homer's myths","Homer as the first teacher of the many kinds of love","Sappho's circle and her phrasing set beside those of Socrates"],"terms":[{"t":"erōtikē technē (amatory art)","d":"The skill Socrates claimed, here likened to a goldsmith's skill in telling true beauty from counterfeit."},{"t":"Diotima","d":"The prophetess Socrates names as his teacher in love, compared here with Sappho."}]},{"i":172,"j":185,"loc":"Dissertation IX","title":"Socrates as shepherd among lovers","summary":"Resuming, Maximus explains that Socrates, unable to curb the corrupt love raging in Athens by law, force or argument, joined the chase in order to outrun the predators. A fable of a lamb choosing a shepherd over a cook pictures Socrates as the shepherd and the other lovers as cooks. True love looks through bodily beauty to the flower of the soul, which beauty of body announces like light on mountain tops before sunrise. Maximus lists the contrasts between the philosopher's open, friendly love and the base lover's secret, destructive desire, which shuns daylight like a thief. He ends praising Agesilaus, who let his love for a beautiful youth go no further than his eyes, as a greater victor than Leonidas.","ideas":["Socrates' love as a stratagem to rescue youths from base lovers","Bodily beauty as the prelude and flower of future virtue","One beauty seen differently by the virtuous and the depraved","Agesilaus's restraint as a victory greater than Thermopylae"],"terms":[{"t":"kallos (beauty)","d":"In the body, the outward bloom of the soul's beauty and promise of virtue."}]},{"i":186,"j":202,"loc":"Dissertation X","title":"Love governed by reason","summary":"Comparing Polycrates' gifts of gold to Smerdies with Anacreon's songs, Maximus prefers love joined with the Muses to love based on power and fear, and calls true love fearless and free. Because evil imitates good, love must be judged by choice, end and virtue; using Plato's division of the soul into reason and passion, he holds that love is a passion that needs reason as its governor, as a horse needs a bridle. Unbridled love leads to the crimes of tragedy; lawful love serves procreation, and reason is the gift that sets man above other animals. Paris and Xerxes show how power without knowledge feeds wanton desire, while Cretan and Spartan customs and the Locrian youth show love that is content to look. He ends likening the lustful man to the Persian king who robbed a tomb for gold.","ideas":["Free, poetic love preferred to tyrannical love","Love is a passion that is healthy only when ruled by reason","Power without reason gives free rein to lust, as with Paris and Xerxes","Love by the eyes alone can be shared, like the sun loved by all"],"terms":[{"t":"pathos (passion)","d":"The non-rational part of the soul; love belongs to it and must be joined with reason."},{"t":"logos (reason)","d":"The governing power given to humans, to which God subjected amatory appetite like a horse to a bridle."}]},{"i":203,"j":217,"loc":"Dissertation XI","title":"A palinode: love is of beauty","summary":"Following Stesichorus's recantation about Helen and Anacreon's later praise of Cleobulus, Maximus retracts the claim that love leads to adultery and lust, which would make a god the cause of impiety. Love is always love of beauty; pursuits of riches, war, tyranny or gold are desire for pleasure, not love. He distinguishes beauty, which must truly be, from pleasure, which needs only to seem; as nourishment is constant while tastes vary with custom from Sicily to Scythia. Love is therefore reason, virtue and art, while desires for pleasure are irrational. Drawing on Socrates' account of recollection, he describes beauty descending from its source through the heavens to earth, growing dimmer, and appearing most fully in man, so that Socrates, like a hunter, pursued the memory of true beauty through bodies.","ideas":["Recantation: love is never the cause of vice","Love aims at real beauty, desire at mere pleasure","Nourishment is natural and fixed, pleasure artificial and varying with custom","Beauty descends like a river into the sea and is recognized through recollection"],"terms":[{"t":"palinode","d":"A recantation, modelled on Stesichorus's retraction of his poem on Helen."},{"t":"anamnēsis (recollection)","d":"The soul's memory of beauty itself, seen before birth and recalled at the sight of beautiful bodies."}]},{"i":218,"j":232,"loc":"Dissertation XII","title":"Philosophic discourse as the soul's true banquet","summary":"Maximus starts from Ulysses' praise of the feast and the harper at Alcinous' court and asks what pleasure really is. He reads Homer as moving pleasure from the belly to the ears, then asks which discourse should feast the ears: melody is like perfume, discourse like food. He rejects forensic oratory and staged mock battles, then gives a long praise of history, which lets the soul travel safely across ages and preserves human deeds. Yet history mixes base with worthy and is full of tyrants and calamities, so a worthy soul needs instead the healthy discourse of Socrates and Plato about the war and disease within the soul. He ends by calling on Apollo and Jupiter as physicians of the diseased soul.","ideas":["Pleasure should pass from the belly to the ears, and from melody to discourse","Forensic rhetoric adulterates truth like slave-dealers who spoil natural bodies","History delights and preserves memory but teaches without discrimination","The soul's inner sedition and disease need a divine physician, not historians"],"terms":[{"t":"harmonia (harmony)","d":"Musical arrangement that pleases the ear but, without discourse, gives no nourishment to the soul."}]},{"i":233,"j":247,"loc":"Dissertation XIII","title":"The case that soldiers serve a city best","summary":"The first of a paired debate argues that soldiers are more useful than husbandmen. Homer praises warriors, not farmers, and Agamemnon is glorified as king and fighter; Lycurgus left farming to Helots and trained free Spartans for war. Maximus runs through Greeks and barbarians to claim that peoples were free while they bore arms and enslaved once they turned to farming, and extends the pattern to animals and to the gods. He argues that nature itself is the real farmer, and that fertile land breeds desire, invasion and war, so agriculture cannot be secure. The speech ends, half in irony, by urging men to leave their land untilled and take up arms.","ideas":["Freedom is won and kept by arms; farming is linked with servitude","Spartan, Athenian, Cretan and Persian history used as evidence","Sun, rain and seasons are the true husbandmen; spontaneous fruits suffice","Rich land excites desire and war, so agriculture is never safe"],"terms":[{"t":"Helots","d":"The Spartan serf class to whom Lycurgus is said to have left farming and manual work."}]},{"i":248,"j":261,"loc":"Dissertation XIV","title":"The reply: husbandmen are more useful than soldiers","summary":"Maximus now argues the other side, setting Hesiod against Homer. War is necessary to the just and voluntary to the unjust, and a just man would wish war abolished as a just physician would wish disease gone. To compare fairly he takes a middle class of men living by right opinion and assigns half to farming, half to arms: war breeds desire, anger, injustice and tyranny, while farming is modest, just, peaceful, and first founded the festivals and mysteries. Farmers are hardier in body, and at Marathon the Athenian farmers fought best; later mercenary armies were beaten, and the Persians conquered while farmers and lost their virtue when they left the plough.","ideas":["War is necessary for the just, chosen by the unjust","Farming fosters temperance, justice and piety; war fosters desire and anger","Husbandmen founded the rites of Bacchus, Ceres and Minerva","Farmer-soldiers at Marathon and Cyrus' Persians outfought professional troops"],"terms":[{"t":"orthē doxa (right opinion)","d":"Following Plato, the state of those neither perfectly virtuous nor wholly vicious, who live by sound belief and training."}]},{"i":262,"j":276,"loc":"Dissertation XV","title":"Speech must match deeds; true pleasure in discourse","summary":"Anacharsis the Scythian searches Greece for a solid wisdom and finds it in Myson of Chena, whose deeds outweigh his words; Pythagoras too spoke briefly but lived continuously by his precepts. Maximus argues that life needs a harmony of words and actions, and that discourse should be judged like a husbandman judges fruit, by its use, not like a traveller praising flowers. Pleasing but useless speech is likened to cookery and to feeding a fever, and he calls instead for a philosophic orator, free of passion, who can counsel, judge and teach. He admits a manly pleasure that follows virtue, like the trumpet's, and closes with Hercules in the fire and Socrates drinking the poison.","ideas":["Words and deeds must be of equal measure","Judge discourse by its fruit in the soul, not by its charm","Flattering rhetoric is cookery for the ears","Virtue has its own spontaneous, inner pleasures"],"terms":[{"t":"Myson of Chena","d":"An obscure villager whom Anacharsis found wise through his works rather than his words."}]},{"i":277,"j":293,"loc":"Dissertation XVI","title":"Homer as the founder of a philosophic school","summary":"Maximus invokes the Muse and portrays Homer's soul as surveying heaven, earth and every human affair. Philosophy, the science of divine and human things, was once veiled in mysteries, fables, music and divination; later thinkers stripped it bare, Homer was dismissed as a fabulist, and rival sects multiplied words without deeds. Plato, he claims, resembles Homer in doctrine more than Socrates. Homer mixes theology, politics and ethics into one fable like many instruments in a single concert: Achilles and Agamemnon against Nestor, Paris against Hector, the threefold division of the cosmos, gods as principles of virtue, love and art, and the battle of fire and water. The Phaeacian and Ithacan cities, and Ulysses saved by virtue, close the section.","ideas":["Ancient wisdom hid philosophy under poetic veils","Homer's poems have a double aim: fable as poetry, virtue and truth as philosophy","Gods in Homer stand for principles; the river battle is a war of elements","Ulysses is an image of virtue opposing art to misfortune"],"terms":[{"t":"moly","d":"The herb Hermes gives Ulysses against Circe, read here as the virtue that preserves him."}]},{"i":294,"j":308,"loc":"Dissertation XVII","title":"Virtue is not an art but its product","summary":"Maximus opposes the view that virtue is simply an art. Every art aims at an end distinct from itself, as medicine aims at health and statuary at a statue; virtue is the health and order of the soul, the work art produces, not the art. He distinguishes productive, practical and theoretical arts, and, citing a Pythagorean doctrine taken up by Plato and Aristotle, divides the soul into reason and passion. Vice is like an ochlocracy where the passions rule; virtue resembles the Spartan constitution in which reason governs the willing passions. So science is wisdom, and virtue is its offspring. Learning alone cannot make men good: natural disposition, education and custom, and finally art are all needed.","ideas":["The end of an art differs from the art itself","Virtue is the health of the soul produced when reason orders passion","The vicious soul is a mob-ruled city; the virtuous soul a Spartan order","Good nature, habituation and art together make a good man"],"terms":[{"t":"ochlocracy","d":"Rule of the mob, used as an image of the soul when passions overpower reason."},{"t":"epistēmē (science)","d":"Rational knowledge, which Maximus calls wisdom and makes the parent rather than the substance of virtue."}]},{"i":309,"j":317,"loc":"Dissertation XVIII","title":"Healing the soul to live without sorrow","summary":"Maximus asks who can be the physician that frees the soul from sorrow. Chiron is his model of one art that cared for both body and soul. He explains that the soul is diffused through the whole body, so pain passes from body to soul and from soul to body, as when grief or love wastes the flesh. Inverting Socrates' remark in the Charmides that the part is healed with the whole, he argues that if the better part, the soul, is healthy, the whole man is well. The art to seek is therefore care of the soul, which either keeps the body well or makes it contemptuous of bodily suffering.","ideas":["Chiron joined medicine of body and soul in one art","The soul pervades the body like light in air","Pain flows both ways between soul and body","Health of the superior part secures the whole"],"terms":[]},{"i":318,"j":333,"loc":"Dissertation XIX","title":"Felicity as the common goal and philosophy's end","summary":"Every people and profession loves something, and Maximus argues that all, even Sardanapalus, Xerxes, Pisistratus, Polycrates, Philip and Alexander, are really pursuing felicity. Common people, idlers, the Ionian juggler and Psaphon with his trained birds do the same. All agree in desiring good, but seek it like people groping for gold in the dark, which fills the world with strife and war. God planted the hope of good but hid its discovery, like Homer's moly; oracles speak of trifles, and only philosophy can foretell how to live. Yet the good is one, while philosophy has split into sects, Pythagoras, Thales, Heraclitus, Socrates, Carneades, Diogenes, Epicurus, and the section ends asking which to follow.","ideas":["All human pursuits aim at felicity, however mistaken","Ignorance of the good causes war and suffering","The good is one, indivisible and sufficient for every rational nature","Rival philosophical sects divide what should be one path"],"terms":[{"t":"eudaimonia (felicity)","d":"Happiness, the end that every human desire claims to seek."}]},{"i":334,"j":349,"loc":"Dissertation XX","title":"The Cynic life compared with civilized life","summary":"Maximus tells a fable: Prometheus at Jupiter's command made men, who first lived simply on the earth's spontaneous gifts, like the golden age under Saturn. A second life followed, with walls, clothes, gold, mining, ships, hunting and meat, and endless contradictory desires and fears. He likens it to prisoners in a dark dungeon who invent pleasures amid groans, and the first life to a free man in pure light. Diogenes of Sinope, freed by Apollo, lived that free life in the iron age, a king whose wealth was the whole earth, healthy without physicians, and safe among warring Greeks. Since every other choice ends in voluntary misery, he judges Diogenes freer even than Socrates, Plato and Xenophon.","ideas":["A golden-age life of nature contrasted with a life of craving and strife","Civilized life as a prison full of mixed groans and songs","Diogenes as a free, unarmed king of the whole earth","Ordinary goals, and even philosophers' lives, are subject to fortune"],"terms":[{"t":"Cynic","d":"Follower of Diogenes' way of life, renouncing property, city and convention for life according to nature."}]},{"i":350,"j":364,"loc":"Dissertation XXI","title":"Music and geometry as aids to virtue","summary":"Maximus recalls Socrates' city in the Republic, educated by music and gymnastic, and the Athenian Stranger in the Laws urging the Cretans to temper courage with music. He objects that virtue seems near and easy, but grants that the many passions of the soul must willingly obey law, which is philosophy. Philosophy then enlists gymnastic, rhetoric, poetry, arithmetic, logistic, geometry and music as helpers. True music, unlike the corrupted music of theatres, harmonizes the soul as the heavens sing, and examples from Pindar, Tyrtaeus, Orpheus, Amphion, Lycurgus and Themistocles show its power over passions and peoples. Geometry likewise is not for land-measuring but, like a ship, carries the mind out to contemplate the heavens and true being.","ideas":["Philosophy is the lawgiver of the soul, with the disciplines as assistants","True music orders the passions; theatrical music corrupts","The music of the spheres and the civic power of song","Geometry is a vehicle lifting the mind to contemplate reality"],"terms":[{"t":"logistic","d":"The art of calculation, distinguished from arithmetic, the study of number itself."},{"t":"music of the spheres","d":"The Pythagorean idea that the ordered revolutions of the heavens produce a divine sound we cannot hear."}]},{"i":365,"j":381,"loc":"Dissertation XXII","title":"Goodness bestowed by divine allotment","summary":"Maximus asks whether one can become good by divine gift rather than art. He tests poetic claims: Homer on Telemachus and Demodocus, Hesiod's call by the Muses, Minos taught by Jupiter, and the self-taught Melesagoras, Epimenides and Aristeas, whose soul supposedly flew round the world. He turns to Socrates, who praised learning yet spoke of divine allotment and of nature as an older teacher. Reason itself argues that god, perfect, self-sufficient and powerful, must give the highest good, virtue, if he gives lesser gifts like prophecy. The best souls, poised between virtue and vice, need divine aid; Ulysses and Hercules were good through trials Jupiter sent, and Socrates left sculpture for virtue by god's choice.","ideas":["Poets and seers claim knowledge without a teacher","A perfect, self-sufficient, powerful god must impart the highest good","Natures divide into aptitude for virtue and for vice","Ulysses' and Hercules' trials were divinely assigned training in virtue"],"terms":[{"t":"theia moira (divine allotment)","d":"A gift or dispensation from god, here proposed as a source of virtue beyond art."}]},{"i":382,"j":395,"loc":"Dissertation XXIII","title":"Argument that no good is greater than another","summary":"Maximus disputes Homer's blame of Glaucus for trading gold armour for bronze, saying friendship rather than value governed the exchange. External things can be measured more and less, but the good is stable, indivisible and admits neither increase nor deficiency. Health, harmony, a choir, a galley, a chariot and a phalanx show that the good in each is one while its contraries are many. Life is a road to Eleusis or Babylon: travellers are nearer or farther, but none has arrived until they reach the good, attained only by a narrow, rough path. Even the gods share one nature under many names. Socrates, poor and despised, was outdone in every external good, yet Apollo named him wisest.","ideas":["Good neither increases nor decreases; only externals admit degrees","Unity marks the good, multiplicity its opposites","Life as a road to the mysteries: nearness is not arrival","Socrates lacked every external good yet was judged wisest"],"terms":[{"t":"Eleusis","d":"Site of the mysteries, used as an image of the good which the uninitiated have not yet entered."}]},{"i":396,"j":411,"loc":"Dissertation XXIV","title":"Counter-argument that good can exceed good","summary":"Taking the opposite side, Maximus has Glaucus defend his exchange and then uses Homer to show that beauty and virtue admit degrees: Venus need not make Juno and Minerva ugly, Diana outshines her nymphs, Agamemnon is fairer than Menelaus, Achilles than Nireus, and valour descends from Achilles through Ajax and Diomed. He tests the claim by utility, possibility and truth: graded goods encourage the many; like is judged against like, as the moon shines only by night; and human life, a blend of soul, body and fortune, ranks them like general, soldier and arms or pilot, ship and winds. He extends the ranking to the senses, the gods and noble birth, and ends by placing wealth among things indifferent.","ideas":["Beauty, valour and virtue have grades without their lesser forms becoming base","Goods must be compared with goods, not with evils","Soul, body and fortune all contribute to felicity but with unequal honour","Wealth is indifferent, at most a means leading to good"],"terms":[{"t":"adiaphora (things indifferent)","d":"Things neither good nor bad, a category in which Maximus places wealth."}]},{"i":412,"j":426,"loc":"Dissertation XXV","title":"Whence evils come if divinity makes good","summary":"Maximus contrasts Alexander's idle question to Ammon about the Nile's source with the question worth asking an oracle for all mankind: where good and evil come from. Good plainly flows from Jupiter, the maker and governor of heaven, earth and sea, whose nod sustains all things. Evils do not come from heaven; they are of two kinds. Some belong to matter and follow necessarily from the making of the whole, like sparks from an anvil, and what we call corruption is the renewal of the universe; others are the soul's own chosen depravity, for which the chooser and not God is to blame. The discourse ends with the soul as a charioteer given reins and free to drive with or without art, dragged off when the unruly horses win.","ideas":["Heaven is free of evil; earth is mixed of good and evil","Physical evils are by-products of cosmic craft, serving the whole, not the artist's aim","Heraclitean cycle: each element lives the death of another","Moral evil comes from the soul's free choice; divinity is blameless"],"terms":[{"t":"matter (hulē)","d":"The material worked by the divine artificer, whose own properties produce the disorder and calamities on earth."},{"t":"charioteer image","d":"Plato's figure of the soul driving a chariot of unruly horses, used to show how the soul may govern or be overcome by passions."}]},{"i":427,"j":443,"loc":"Dissertation XXVI","title":"What the daemon of Socrates was","summary":"Maximus asks why anyone should doubt that a divine power attended Socrates when no one doubts the Pythia, Dodona, Trophonius' cave, or the oracle of the dead near Aornos. Before asking what the daemon was, one must grant that daemons exist; Homer shows Athena, Hera and others guiding Achilles, Diomed and Telemachus, and these names and images signify real divine powers. Divinity helps the good through oracles, dreams and voices because the soul is weak and fortune clouds virtue as clouds hide the sun. Below the supreme god are secondary immortals, the daemons, set between heaven and earth as interpreters between gods and men, numerous and varied as human characters; only a depraved soul lacks one.","ideas":["Socrates' daemon was single, private, and restraining, unlike Homer's many-named powers","Statues and myths are enigmas pointing to real divine powers","Fortune obscures virtue, so the good need divine aid","Daemons mediate between gods and men like interpreters between languages"],"terms":[{"t":"daimonion (daemoniacal power)","d":"The divine sign or guardian spirit that attended Socrates and warned him away from certain actions."},{"t":"daemons","d":"Secondary immortal beings between gods and men, ministers of the gods and guardians of humans."}]},{"i":444,"j":456,"loc":"Dissertation XXVII","title":"The nature of daemons: immortal yet passive","summary":"Continuing the inquiry, Maximus argues that nature fills the scale of being with middle terms, just as air and water reconcile fire and earth and media join extremes in harmony and the body. God is impassive and immortal, man passive and mortal, so the mean between them must be immortal and passive: the daemon. Daemons are immortal because the soul contains the body and is not itself contained; a soul freed from the body becomes a daemon, sees beauty itself, and is sent back to help the good and punish the unjust. Their passivity lies in keeping earthly inclinations, so Asclepius still heals and Achilles still bears arms; Maximus reports Achilles' island in the Pontus and says he himself has seen the Dioscuri, Asclepius and Hercules while awake.","ideas":["Nature never leaps between extremes without a mean","Daemon = immortal and passive, the link between god and man","Disembodied good souls become daemons who aid mankind","Heroes keep their earthly crafts and appear to people"],"terms":[{"t":"impassive (apathēs)","d":"Not subject to being acted on or affected; the property that marks God off from daemons."},{"t":"mean / medium","d":"A middle term sharing a quality with each extreme, which allows contraries to be joined."}]},{"i":457,"j":474,"loc":"Dissertation XXVIII","title":"Whether learning is recollection","summary":"Maximus reads the tales of Epimenides' long sleep, Pythagoras recognizing his shield as Euphorbus, and Aristeas' soul roaming free as images of a soul withdrawn from bodily tumult that meets truth again. Learning and Plato's reminiscence are one thing: as art clears an eye without giving it sight, reason acts as midwife to the soul, which is pregnant with true seeds that must always have been there and so are immortal. The regress of teachers shows knowledge must be innate, and reason marshals sleeping conceptions as Agamemnon orders his troops. Sense supplies a small start from which intellect follows chains of association, as with Ulysses weeping at Demodocus' song; Socrates' questioning only steadies what the learner already has. The soul recovers full memory above the heavens, and Mnemosyne as mother of the Muses means this.","ideas":["Earthly life as a dream; pure souls dream through the gate of horn","Teaching removes obstacles; it does not implant knowledge","Oblivion, reminiscence and memory as sleep, waking and guarding of conceptions","Association by time, passion, place, distribution and power"],"terms":[{"t":"anamnēsis (reminiscence)","d":"Plato's doctrine that learning is the soul's recollection of knowledge it already possesses."},{"t":"maieutic art (obstetric art)","d":"Socrates' midwifery, drawing out the conceptions the soul already carries."},{"t":"Mnemosyne","d":"Memory, mother of the Muses, read as the source of the sciences."}]},{"i":475,"j":487,"loc":"Dissertation XXIX","title":"Poets and philosophers on the gods","summary":"Maximus holds that poetry and philosophy are one thing differing only in age and form: poetry is ancient, metrical philosophy, and philosophy is newer, plainer poetry. As Asclepius might say of old and new medicine, simpler souls once needed gentle fables, while later, more sceptical souls demanded naked argument. Neither Homer nor Plato literally saw gods at feasts or Plato's rivers of the underworld; both speak in enigmas, and myth sits between knowledge and ignorance, leading the soul on like a sweetened medicine. Rename the gods and the doctrines match, Jupiter as first intellect, Minerva as wisdom, Apollo as the sun; only Epicurus, whose idle gods care for nothing, is excluded from both camps.","ideas":["Poetry and philosophy share one essence, differing in time and style","Myths are enigmatic veils, like coverings on statues in the mysteries","Homeric gods translated into philosophical principles","Epicurus' idle gods are more absurd than any fable"],"terms":[{"t":"muthos (fable)","d":"A discourse dressed in foreign ornament, clearer than an enigma but less plain than direct statement."},{"t":"allegory / enigma","d":"Speech that hides a truth beneath an image, to be decoded by the inquirer."}]},{"i":488,"j":504,"loc":"Dissertation XXX","title":"Whether one ought to pray","summary":"Midas' golden wish and Croesus' misread oracle show foolish prayers, and Homeric scenes in which gods seem moved by sacrifice raise the question whether divinity can be bent. Maximus argues it cannot: the deserving will receive without praying and the undeserving will not receive by praying. What men pray for falls under providence, fate, fortune or art; providence cares for the whole and gives what is fitting unasked, fate is an inflexible tyrant even to Jupiter, fortune is senseless, and art already holds what it needs. Socrates and other philosophers prayed constantly, but their prayer was a conversation with the gods and an exercise of virtue, not a request for wealth; philosophy alone knows how to pray.","ideas":["Divinity does not change its mind or take bribes","Fourfold division: providence, fate, fortune, art","The universe's physician sacrifices parts for the whole","Philosophic prayer as converse with the gods, not petition"],"terms":[{"t":"pronoia (providence)","d":"The work of divinity, caring for the whole and perhaps for particulars."},{"t":"heimarmenē (fate)","d":"Necessity, personified by the three Fates, which no prayer or gift can move."},{"t":"tuchē (fortune)","d":"The irrational, unstable source of chance gifts, not worth praying to."}]},{"i":505,"j":525,"loc":"Dissertations XXXI–XXXII","title":"Pleasure unstable, and man's good","summary":"Since goodness is hard for man alone, Maximus compares guides in philosophy to pilots and sets Epicurus' doctrine beside the luxurious pleasure-ship of a barbarian king that broke up in the first storm while plain ships survived. Granting for argument that pleasure is good, he denies it is stable: pleasure is always mixed with pain, as the suitors, Paris, Sybaris and Polycrates show. Dissertation XXXII corrects the earlier concession: what is good must be stable, so unstable pleasure is only a counterfeit good. Each animal's good lies in its proper work and the power that preserves it, and man's is intellect, not the pleasure shared with oxen and apes, whose organs lead only to gluttony and excess.","ideas":["Epicureanism as a splendid but unseaworthy pleasure-ship","Pleasure and pain are bound together and alternate","The good must be stable; apparent goods are counterfeit coin","Man's good is found where his proper work is: intellect"],"terms":[{"t":"hēdonē (pleasure)","d":"The Epicurean candidate for the good, which Maximus argues is unstable and common to all animals."},{"t":"ergon (proper work)","d":"The function peculiar to a kind of being, in which its good consists."}]},{"i":526,"j":540,"loc":"Dissertation XXXIII","title":"A defence of pleasure","summary":"Here Maximus argues the opposing side, using Aesop's fable of the shepherd who betrayed the stag to suggest that pleasure's accusers secretly follow it. Pleasure is innate and older than reason or art, the foundation of every animal's survival; the luxuries blamed on it are the products of art and reason misused. Even virtue is chosen for the pleasure it brings, and the labours of Achilles, Hercules and Spartan discipline are paid for in pleasure; Socrates loved beautiful youths, not plain ones. Diogenes is the most exact lover of pleasure, enjoying tub and sunlight without the pains of kings, and Athens' festivals and Sparta's freedom from fear show pleasures on a civic scale.","ideas":["Pleasure is natural, self-taught and necessary for survival","Luxury is the fault of art and reason, not of pleasure","Virtue and noble labours are chosen for the pleasure they bring","Diogenes' painless pleasures outdo those of Xerxes and Croesus"],"terms":[{"t":"chara (joy)","d":"A name some give to good pleasure; the speaker says it is the same thing renamed."}]},{"i":541,"j":552,"loc":"Dissertation XXXIV","title":"The end of philosophy: reason over pleasure","summary":"Philosophy abounds in equal opposing arguments, and in the contest of pleasure against virtue pleasure wins in opinion, numbers and passion, leaving only reason on virtue's side. Maximus rejects the Epicurean who keeps the name of philosopher while serving pleasure; pleasure may follow good conduct but must not rule, for as ruler it is an insatiable tyrant. Natural needs are met by springs and wild food, but going beyond need breeds the endless craving of the Persian king, which drove Xerxes into Europe, and the torment of Tantalus. Man stands between mortal and immortal; his saving part is the soul, its instrument intellect and its work prudence, which is man's good, while those ruled by appetite become Centaurs, beasts in human form.","ideas":["Pleasure as follower, not ruler; as ruler a tyrant","Desire beyond natural need is boundless and painful","Soul saves body; intellect is its instrument; prudence its work","Centaurs and chimaeras as images of appetite ruling the soul"],"terms":[{"t":"telos (end)","d":"The goal of philosophy and of human life, here identified with the work of intellect."},{"t":"phronēsis (prudence)","d":"The work of intellect and, Maximus concludes, the good of man."}]},{"i":553,"j":564,"loc":"Dissertation XXXV","title":"Advantage from adverse circumstances","summary":"Maximus argues that good is never given to humans unmixed, any more than the eye could endure endless daylight. He reinterprets Homer's two jars of Zeus as the soul's virtue and depravity; virtue's stream, like the Nile with the farmer's labour, yields fruit only with toil. Pilots and generals tested by misfortune are trustworthy, while long prosperity, as with Croesus and Polycrates, is precarious; contrast sharpens pleasure, as the thirsting Persian king learned. Achilles, Ulysses and Hercules won fame only through hardships, and the true contests of virtue belong to Socrates, Plato, Xenophon and Diogenes, and to Marathon and Sparta.","ideas":["Good and evil are mixed in human life by necessity","The two jars read as virtue and vice","Satiety of prosperity is worse than satiety of food","Hardship is the antagonist that makes virtue's crown possible"],"terms":[{"t":"the two jars (pithoi)","d":"Homer's jars of good and evil at Zeus's threshold, read here as virtue and depravity."}]},{"i":565,"j":571,"loc":"Dissertation XXXVI 1.1–1.3","title":"Philosophical discourse fits every occasion","summary":"As actors play many roles, the philosopher acting in life's drama, composed by divinity, must adapt his speech to every circumstance without being thought a mere Proteus. A musician who could play only one mode would be useless; the more virile muse of philosophy must command all modes because human affairs are ever changing. Life looks like one river but is constantly renewed and many-formed, and reason presides over it as medicine over the shifting body. Unlike harper, orator or poet, the philosopher has no special season, for reason is bound up with life as light with the eye.","ideas":["Life as a drama written by divinity","Philosophy must master every mode of speech","Life is like a river, seemingly one but always changing","Reason as shepherd of the human herd"],"terms":[{"t":"logos (reason / discourse)","d":"Both the faculty that governs life and the philosopher's speech fitted to each occasion."}]},{"i":572,"j":584,"loc":"Dissertation XXXVI 1.4–1.10","title":"The soul's contest and the true philosopher","summary":"Maximus compares philosophical discourse to athletic games, but in this contest of the soul spectators should want to be competitors, and the champion wins most by drawing others in. Love and hope spur the soul; aimed at wealth they are endless, but aimed at the stable good they win. He complains that hearers praise arguments without living by them, and offers his own varied speech to lovers of rhetoric, poetry and politics. True philosophy is not words, refutations and sophisms but a guide who trains the young as a horseman trains colts; a philosopher is known by his mind, not his garb or fortune, as Pythagoras, Socrates, Xenophon and Diogenes each played their role.","ideas":["Contests of the soul are open to nearly all, unlike bodily ones","Love and hope as the wings and comfort of the soul's impulses","Praise without imitation is empty","Fortune's dress is a costume; the discourse is what matters"],"terms":[{"t":"hormē (impulse)","d":"The soul's drive toward its objects, which philosophers call the wing given by love."}]},{"i":585,"j":596,"loc":"Dissertation XXXVII","title":"How to prepare for friendship","summary":"Homer's godlike heroes resemble Jupiter not in his amours but in imitating his saving, paternal care, which among men is called friendship and benevolence. Human friendship is rare: trade, avarice and distrust have buried it, Homer's whole war yields only Achilles and Patroclus, and Athens only Harmodius and Aristogeiton against the tyrant. Gold, beauty, glory and fear of prison and death all distract men from it, and flatterers counterfeit it. States make thirty-year truces and keep festivals while hating one another, and a private war of envy and anger rages in the soul; only philosophy can proclaim the true peace that friendship needs.","ideas":["Likeness to Zeus lies in his paternal, saving love","Friendship is a rare, priceless possession","Greek history is mostly strife among kin","Friendship requires peace within the soul"],"terms":[{"t":"philia (friendship)","d":"The human form of the divine virtue the gods call Themis and Justice."}]},{"i":597,"j":610,"loc":"Dissertation XXXVIII","title":"Whether statues should be dedicated to gods","summary":"People honour gods with fitting dedications: sailors with helms, shepherds with caves, peoples with rivers and mountains. The divine needs no statues, but weak humans need symbols as aids to memory, like a schoolboy's tracing letters. The Greeks rightly use the human form, nearest to the soul's likeness to God; Maximus faults Persian fire-worship and Egyptian animal cults, tells of a crocodile-god eating its foster-brother and an Indian dragon worshipped as Bacchus, and lists Libyan Atlas, the Celtic oak, the Arabian stone and the Phrygian rivers. Since every people uses symbols, they should be kept: God, ineffable and invisible, is approached through images as lovers treasure keepsakes.","ideas":["Statues serve human weakness, not divine need","Survey of Greek and barbarian symbols of the gods","The supreme god is nameless and unseen, reached through images","Let all peoples only know, love and remember the divine"],"terms":[{"t":"agalma (statue)","d":"A cult image or symbol of a god, from a carved figure to a mountain or river."}]},{"i":611,"j":622,"loc":"Dissertation XXXIX","title":"Socrates was right not to defend himself","summary":"Unlike other experts, Socrates is still judged by the ignorant crowd although Apollo praised his wisdom. Maximus imagines Socrates winning with a conventional, tearful defence and says philosophy could not have welcomed such a victor. Arguing before unjust, ignorant judges about virtue and the gods would be like an old man quarrelling with boys over dice, and fear of death would mean deserting his post; free speech would only have inflamed them. His silence was like Leonidas standing firm against Xerxes, and Maximus turns the indictment back on Athens, impious and a corrupter of youth, condemned by plague and defeat.","ideas":["Virtue's only defence is reverence for it, absent in that court","A begging apology would be flight from one's post","Socrates' silence as Leonidas' stand","The indictment reversed against the Athenians"],"terms":[{"t":"apologia (defence speech)","d":"A formal reply to charges in court, which Maximus argues Socrates rightly refused to make in the usual way."}]},{"i":623,"j":635,"loc":"Dissertation XL","title":"What science is","summary":"Man surpasses beasts by science and falls short of the gods by folly; divine and human knowledge differ as eternal from brief life. Maximus first considers science as experience collected by sense, as in the origins of sailing and medicine, but cranes carrying stones as ballast and stags swimming in file show beasts have experience too. So man's mark is reason, and science is reason steadily ordering its objects, as in arithmetic, geometry and music. The soul has sense, prudence and intellect, and their harmony is science; intellect is like the unwritten divine law, under which Socrates was a philosopher and Alcibiades was exiled from philosophy, while Minos, Cyrus and Lycurgus show royal science.","ideas":["Experience alone cannot define science; animals share it","Science as the stable, ordering activity of reason","Sense, prudence and intellect, likened to builder, architect, geometer","Divine unwritten law versus the city's laws"],"terms":[{"t":"epistēmē (science)","d":"Scientific knowledge, defined as the harmony of sense, prudence and intellect."},{"t":"empeiria (experience)","d":"Knowledge gathered by sense from repeated encounters, the start of the arts."},{"t":"nous (intellect)","d":"The highest, ruling part of the soul, compared to the divine unwritten law."}]},{"i":636,"j":650,"loc":"Dissertation XLI","title":"Diseases of body versus diseases of soul","summary":"Starting from a hymn to Health, Maximus asks whose health is meant and compares soul and body to ruler and people in a city. The ruler's disease does more harm, so disease of the soul, depravity, which is voluntary and hated, is worse than bodily calamity, which is involuntary and pitied. Pericles kept Athens alive through plague, while the sick souls of Cleon and Alcibiades later ruined a healthy people. A strong soul despises bodily illness, like Pherecydes or the diseased Philoctetes whom Troy's fall required, whereas the healthy bodies of Sardanapalus, Alcibiades and Critias served souls diseased past cure.","ideas":["Soul to body as ruler to people","Depravity is voluntary and spreads to neighbours; bodily disease does not","Pericles as the healthy soul of plague-struck Athens","Philoctetes: sick body, sound soul, needed for victory"],"terms":[{"t":"kakia (depravity)","d":"The disease of the soul, voluntary and harder to cure than any bodily illness."}]}]});